<interrogationes unitas="pseudoterminale">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I run a program in a pseudo-terminal and talk to it?
    <responsum symbolum="pseudoterminale_configuratio_initiare pseudoterminale_posix_creare Pseudoterminale" via="lib/terminale.c">Fill
      a PseudoterminaleConfiguratio with
      pseudoterminale_configuratio_initiare, set argumenta (a
      NIHIL-terminated argv, run through PATH with execvp - no shell),
      then call pseudoterminale_posix_creare. You get back a
      Pseudoterminale: a table of function pointers (legere, scribere,
      amplitudo, finitus, fossa, claudere) that you call with
      pt-&gt;datum as the first argument, e.g.
      pt-&gt;legere(pt-&gt;datum, buffer, n, mora_ms). The child gets a
      new session with the slave as its controlling terminal and as
      fds 0, 1 and 2, so stdout and stderr arrive mixed on one
      stream.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I tell "the binary does not exist" apart from a child that exited with 127?
    <responsum symbolum="pseudoterminale_posix_creare pseudoterminale_error_nomen PseudoterminaleError">pseudoterminale_posix_creare
      waits on a CLOEXEC pipe until exec either succeeds (the pipe
      closes) or fails (the child writes the step and errno). A failed
      chdir or exec returns NIHIL with *error =
      PSEUDOTERMINALE_ERROR_EXEC and *descriptio such as "exec: No such
      file or directory"; the half-born child is already reaped. A
      real program that later exits 127 shows up instead through
      finitus with codex 127. One gap: if the child's dup2 onto fds
      0-2 fails it exits 127 without writing to the pipe, so creare
      succeeds and the failure looks like an ordinary exit 127. Both
      error and descriptio may be NIHIL; pseudoterminale_error_nomen
      gives a fixed Latin name for the error code.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can scribere block if the child stops reading its input?
    <responsum symbolum="pseudoterminale_posix_creare Pseudoterminale">No.
      The master fd is set O_NONBLOCK, and scribere returns how many
      bytes the kernel accepted: possibly 0 (EAGAIN), possibly fewer
      than you asked, -1 only on a real error. The host must keep the
      rest and send it later. The worklog measured a raw-mode child
      that never reads at 1022 accepted bytes; a canonical-mode child
      accepts everything (macOS discards past MAX_INPUT).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I wait for output from the child in my own event loop?
    <responsum symbolum="Pseudoterminale pseudoterminale_posix_creare">Call
      pt-&gt;fossa(pt-&gt;datum) to get the master descriptor and put it
      in your poll set; never read or close it yourself. When it is
      readable call legere: mora_ms 0 does not wait, a positive value
      waits that many milliseconds, a negative value waits forever.
      legere returns the bytes read, 0 when nothing arrived in time
      (or EINTR/EAGAIN), and -1 when the child closed the slave side
      (read 0 or EIO). The memoriae bridge returns -1 from fossa,
      since it has no descriptor.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find out whether the child exited and with what status?
    <responsum symbolum="PseudoterminaleExitus Pseudoterminale">Call
      pt-&gt;finitus(pt-&gt;datum, &amp;exitus): it reaps with waitpid
      WNOHANG, so it never blocks. It returns FALSUM while the child
      runs and VERUM once it has exited; later calls return VERUM and
      the same exitus. exitus.codex is WEXITSTATUS (0 when the child
      was killed) and exitus.signum is WTERMSIG or 0. If someone else
      reaped the pid (ECHILD) it still reports VERUM, but the exit is
      unknown and exitus reads codex 0, signum 0, which looks like a
      clean exit. exitus may be NIHIL for the posix bridge but not for
      the memoriae one. There are no signal handlers: the host polls
      finitus, typically after legere returned -1.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I skip claudere if the child has already exited?
    <responsum symbolum="Pseudoterminale pseudoterminale_posix_creare">No:
      claudere must always be called, and only it closes the master
      fd. It sends SIGHUP to the child's process group and the child
      (only if not yet reaped, so a reused pid is never signalled),
      closes the master, polls waitpid for up to XX steps of V ms,
      then sends SIGKILL and waits. Afterwards there is no zombie and
      no open fd, even for a child that ignores SIGHUP. It is
      idempotent.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I set or remove environment variables for the child without losing TERM?
    <responsum symbolum="pseudoterminale_configuratio_initiare PseudoterminaleConfiguratio" via="lib/terminale.c">cfg.ambitus
      is a NIHIL-terminated list of changes applied on top of the
      inherited environment: "NAME=value" sets, a bare "NAME" unsets.
      The default from pseudoterminale_configuratio_initiare is
      { "TERM=xterm-256color", "COLORTERM=truecolor" }. Assigning your
      own array REPLACES that list, so include the TERM and COLORTERM
      lines yourself if you want them (lib/terminale.c passes its own
      ambitus_terminalis). A name of CCLV characters or more is
      silently skipped.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I resize the terminal the child sees?
    <responsum symbolum="Pseudoterminale PseudoterminaleConfiguratio">Call
      pt-&gt;amplitudo(pt-&gt;datum, cols, rows, px_w, px_h): it sets
      TIOCSWINSZ on the master and the kernel sends SIGWINCH to the
      child. Pixel sizes may be 0 (unknown). It returns FALSUM after
      claudere, for a size below I, or above 0xFFFF. The starting size
      comes from cfg.latitudo and cfg.altitudo (LXXX x XXIV by
      default) and is checked the same way: a bad size makes creare
      fail with PSEUDOTERMINALE_ERROR_ARGUMENTA.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I test a terminal host without spawning a real process?
    <responsum symbolum="pseudoterminale_memoriae_creare pseudoterminale_memoriae_captum pseudoterminale_memoriae_amplitudo" via="probationes/probatio_pseudoterminale.c">Use
      the memoriae bridge, the test seam: pseudoterminale_memoriae_creare
      takes a canned output that legere returns (then -1), and an exit
      code that finitus reports once all output has been read.
      Everything the host writes is captured and visible through
      pseudoterminale_memoriae_captum (a view, valid until the next
      scribere); pseudoterminale_memoriae_amplitudo reports the last
      size set. Because Pseudoterminale is a function table, host
      code calls both bridges the same way. They are not identical:
      the memoriae legere ignores mora_ms, its fossa is -1, its tty is
      NIHIL, its amplitudo has no 0xFFFF limit, and its finitus
      writes through exitus with no NIHIL check.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the tty field of a Pseudoterminale for?
    <responsum symbolum="Pseudoterminale pseudoterminale_posix_creare" via="lib/terminale.c">It
      is the slave device name returned by openpty, such as
      "/dev/ttys011", NUL-terminated and copied into the piscina.
      It was added for the colloquia plan (T4c) because tmux
      identifies its clients by tty; lib/terminale.c copies it into
      the terminal's state. It is NIHIL for the memoriae
      bridge.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can signals my host ignores or blocks leak into the child program?
    <responsum symbolum="pseudoterminale_posix_creare" via="lib/pseudoterminale.worklog.md lib/pseudoterminale_posix.c">Partly.
      A disposition set to SIG_IGN survives exec, so the child resets
      HUP, INT, QUIT, PIPE, ALRM, TERM, CHLD, TSTP, TTIN, TTOU and
      WINCH to SIG_DFL before execvp (a host that ignores SIGPIPE does
      not pass that on). Any other signal the host ignores stays
      ignored, and the signal MASK is not touched: anything the host
      blocked with sigprocmask is still blocked in the program running
      in the terminal.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
